tion\n   [?x ...] yields one binding per element; relation [[?a ?b]] one per\n   tuple. Terms unify as in patterns, so _ is blank and a bare symbol is a\n   constant. A collection or relation form takes a sequential or a set ret\n   (order is irrelevant: the result is a set); nil or an empty one yields\n   no binding, and anything else, a map included, is a query error.\"\n  [binding out ret clause]\n  (if-not (vector? out)\n    (if-let [b (unify binding out ret)] [b] [])\n    (let [kind (classify-in-pattern out)\n          terms (case kind\n                  :coll (butlast out)\n                  :relation (first out)\n                  :tuple out)]\n      (when (or (and (= :coll kind) (not= 2 (count out)))\n                (and (= :relation kind) (not= 1 (count out)))\n                (some #(or (= '... %) (vector? %)) terms))\n        (throw (ex-info\n                 \"Unsupported binding form — use ?x, [?a ?b], [?x ...] or [[?a ?b]]\"\n                 {:binding-form out, :clause clause})))\n      (when (and (#{:coll :relation} kind)\n                 (not (or (nil? ret) (sequential? ret) (set? ret))))\n        (throw (ex-info (str \"Function return for a \"\n                             (if (= :coll kind) \"collection\" \"relation\")\n                             \" binding must be nil, sequential or a set\")\n                        {:binding-form out, :returned ret, :clause clause})))\n      (case kind\n        :tuple (if-let [b (unify-tuple binding out ret clause)] [b] [])\n        :coll (let [term (first out)]\n                (into [] (keep #(unify binding term %)) ret))\n        :relation (into [] (keep #(unify-tuple binding terms % clause)) ret)))))\n\n\n(defn- eval-fn-clause\n  [clause binding ctx]\n  (let [[fsym-and-args & result-vars] clause\n        fsym (first fsym-and-args)\n        args (rest fsym-and-args)\n        special (eval-special-fn fsym args binding)]\n    (if special\n      (let [{:keys [ret]} special]\n        (when (> (count result-vars) 1)\n          (throw\n            (ex-info\n              \"Function clause takes one binding form; use a tuple [?a ?b] for multi-return\"\n              {:clause clause})))\n        (if (empty? result-vars)\n2:description: Team roster, role routing, and reviewer-independence rules for datom.world\n7:Canonical guide for the shared roster, role routing, and reviewer independence.\n18:backed by one file, `roles/reviewer.md`, covering adversarial, routine, and\n19:security-sign-off review via its Adversarial Review and Consensus Follow-up\n33:| [Review](roles/reviewer.md)                       | Adversarial defect discovery, routine correctness/portability checks, and security capability sign-off |\n57:|                 |                                        | (Architect & security sign-offs). Do not assign general implementation or routine reviews.                   |\n61:| codex           | Flat: ChatGPT Plus ($20/mo)            | Strict rate limits. Reserved strictly for Architectural Review roles. Do not assign implementation tasks.   |\n80:| `claude-fable-5-1`          | Highest capability for long-horizon agentic work, complex research,| Slower inference; resource hog (rate limits); mandatory             |\n82:|                             | **Best for:** Architecture definition, security sign-offs.         | one-shot implementations where strict rigor is overkill.            |\n84:| `claude-opus-5.5`           | Performs at Fable 5.1 levels for most work at markedly lower cost  | Still higher latency/cost than Sonnet 5. **Avoid for:** Fast QA     |\n90:|                             | high cost-efficiency. **Best for:** Core implementation, deep QA   | architectural sign-offs on high-risk cryptographic or consensus     |\n91:|                             | adversarial code reviews, multi-file refactors, and test suites.   | boundaries where Opus 5.5 / Fable 5.1 / Astra is mandated.          |\n98:|                             | workflows and adversarial reviews.                                 | relative to size. **Avoid for:** General use (use Flash instead).   |\n104:| `gemini-4-argon`            | Frontier reasoning with configurable effort and deep synthesis     | Higher latency/cost at medium/high effort than Flash-tier models;   |\n107:|                             | **Best for:** System architecture, design/code review, complex     | headless tasks requiring in-session JVM test execution.             |\n108:|                             | debugging, and multi-step implementation.                          |                                                                     |\n123:| `gpt-6-astra`               | Most capable end-to-end model. **Best for:** Hardest architecture, | Highest cost. Reserve for the highest-risk or most difficult work   |\n125:|                             | Capability includes implementation, but the current codex policy   | unsuitable for implementation.                                      |\n128:| `gpt-6-luna`                | Most affordable GPT-6 model; optimized for high-volume tasks and   | Low-cost does not mean suitable for unbounded design or the hardest |\n132:| `gpt-6.1-sol`               | Intelligent everyday workhorse with near-Astra intelligence, 1M+   | More expensive than Luna. Use Astra when the task is the hardest    |\n135:|                             | predecessor. **Best for:** Complex implementation, advanced coding,|                                                                     |\n136:|                             | high-stakes security, and architectural review.                    |                                                                     |\n138:| `gpt-6-terra`               | Balanced intelligence and cost. **Best for:** Default production   | Use Sol or Astra for the hardest reasoning, high-risk security, or  |\n140:|                             | moderate-complexity implementation, and subagent fallback.         | specialist work with focused tests and independent review.          |\n171:|                             | scale reviews.                                                     | searches or quick edits.                                            |\n182:Every change must be reviewed by a model from a **different family** to ensure\n183:independent defect discovery. Never silently substitute a same-family reviewer.\n186:Any author from one family must be reviewed by a model from a different family.\n188:Routine review applies only when independent; architectural or security review\n2:description: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n12:bb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\n13:bb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n14:# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\n15:bb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\n16:bb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\n17:bb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\n18:bb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\n19:bb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n20:bb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n21:bb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\n22:bb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n23:bb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n24:# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n31:# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n45:# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n47:# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n49:# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n50:# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n57:# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n59:# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\n63:# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n65:# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\n73:### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n75:`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\n84:needed builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n85:`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\n95:### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n97:`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\n102:bb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\n103:bb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\n104:bb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\n105:bb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\n106:bb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\n107:bb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\n121:and lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\n126:1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n127:2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n128:3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n130:5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n157:clj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n160:clj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n163:The Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n164:[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\n168:`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\n170:each of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\n171:script compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n172:<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n175:under `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\n179:`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\n182:stale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\n190:- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n193:- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n236:shards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\n258:  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
